      "text": "First, we rewrite the equation using the change of base formula: $\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}$.\n\nNext, we simplify the denominator: $\\log 27 = \\log 3^3 = 3\\log 3$. Now the equation becomes $\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log x} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3}$.\n\nSince $\\log 4 = 2\\log 2$, we can rewrite the equation as $\\frac{2\\log 2}{\\log x} = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\nCross-multiplying gives us $6\\log 2 = \\log x$. Using the property $\\log a^b = b\\log a$, we have $\\log x = \\log 6^2 = \\log 36$.\n\nTherefore, $x = \\boxed{36}$. \n\nLet's verify the solution:\nWe plug $x = 36$ into the original equation: $\\log_{36} 4 = \\log_{27} 3$.\n\nUsing the change of base formula again, we get $\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log 36} = \\frac{\\log 3}{\\log 27}$.\n\nSimplifying the denominator, we have $\\frac{\\log 4}{\\log (6^2)} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3}$.\n\nUsing the property $\\log a^b = b\\log a$, we get $\\frac{\\log 4}{2\\log 6} = \\frac{\\log 3}{3\\log 3}$.\n\nSimplifying further, we have $\\frac{2\\log 2}{2\\log 6} = \\frac{1}{3}$, which is true.\n\nThus, the solution $x = 36$ is correct.",
